{-
   The unsafe layer, and where the rest of it is.

   `Pointer.yana` is the pointer itself and the machine operations written over one. Beside it:
   `Memory.yana` (the block fill, copy and move ladders), `Heap.yana` (the region chain and the free
   lists), `Run.yana` (the allocation primitive every container is built on, and the slice), and
   `Text.yana` (what a native `String` is made of).

   And **two platform files, of which a build compiles one**: `Linux.yana` on the native target and
   `Host.yana` on JS. They sit here rather than in modules of their own because each is written in
   terms of the rest of this module and the rest of this module is written in terms of it - which is
   what a module is for, and what an import cannot do.
-}

{-
   Raw pointers.
  
   Every operation below is unsafe by construction: a raw pointer carries no lifetime, no
   exclusivity, and no promise that what it points at is initialized or still exists. Taking one
   of a value gives its owner writable, stable-address representation requirements, since the
   memory a pointer names is always mutable.
-}

-- Reads what a pointer points at. Written `*p`.
@builtin(pointerLoad)
pub fn *(it: %a) -> a

-- Reads what a device pointer points at. The access is observable and is never folded, merged or
-- removed by the compiler. Volatile is deliberately an operation rather than a pointer type: the
-- same address may be ordinary memory in one access and device memory in another.
@builtin(volatileLoad)
pub fn volatileLoad(it: %a) -> a

-- Writes through a pointer. There is no assignment form for this because `*p = v` is already one:
-- a dereference in assignment position names storage rather than producing a value.
--
-- `->value`, because the value is what the pointee holds afterwards: the frame that owned it stops
-- owing it a drop here, and this declaration is the only thing that says so - an intrinsic has no
-- body for the ownership pass to read, so a hand-over it does not write down is one nothing sees.
@builtin(pointerStore)
pub fn store(to: %a, ->value: a) -> {}

-- Writes device memory. This is volatile, but not atomic; use the Atomic operations when the
-- location also needs indivisible access and an explicit memory ordering.
@builtin(volatileStore)
pub fn volatileStore(to: %a, ->value: a) -> {}

{-
   The address of a value. A value this is applied to cannot stay in a register, so it is what
   forces storage to exist for something that would otherwise have had none.

   **The parameter publishes its pointee** - `it: source'a` and `-> %source'a`, the same qualifier
   `+`, `-`, `borrow` and `values` carry. It is what is true: the pointer names the argument's own
   storage, so a borrow taken through it is a borrow of that storage and the argument has to outlive
   the pointer. Written `it: a` the result was a pointer into nothing a signature could name, and the
   escape rule at a signature had nothing to follow - which is what
   `test/resolve/ownership/Reject.Pointer.yana` used to state as a cost.

   `compiler/resolve`, deleted in J173, **read none of this**: it supplied its own definition of the
   role (`readIntrinsicRole`), so a trivially copyable argument was still copied into the call and
   the address was the copy's, which is what `test/resolve/Global.yana` documented as the meaning of
   `addressOf` of a constant there. The two stages disagreed about that one program, deliberately,
   and this file states what the *language* means.
-}
@builtin(addressOf)
pub fn addressOf(it: source'a) -> %source'a

{-
   Reinterpreting a pointer - which is `Core.bitcast`, and not three functions of this module's own.

   `cast(p)`, `asInt(p)` and `asPtr(n)` would be three spellings of one operation, and none of them
   the spelling anything else in the language uses. The instances below are the whole surface:
   `bitcast(p) :: %b` between two pointer types, and `bitcast(p) :: I64` and `bitcast(n) :: %a`
   between an address and the integer holding it.

   The one thing this loses is the qualifier. Instance visibility is program-wide, so these three
   rungs make `bitcast` reach a pointer from every module of a program in which *any* module
   imported Native, where `Native.cast` had to be named through the module and so said "unsafe" at
   the call site. That weakening was taken deliberately: reinterpretation is one idea, and having
   one name for it was judged worth more than a qualifier that only some of the reinterpretations
   ever carried.

   Declared here as generated instances rather than in source, because each is one instruction and
   `%a` is a type variable - `definePointerInstances` is where they are, alongside `Eq` and `Ord`.
-}

{-
   The null pointer, and the test for it.

   **Written here rather than supplied by the compiler**, and that is a statement about the language
   rather than an economy. Address zero is a perfectly ordinary address on bare hardware and inside a
   kernel, so "the null pointer" is a convention *this module* holds and not a fact the core language
   knows - and a compiler that built the constant itself would be a compiler with an opinion about
   the target's address space. The two rungs these are written over are the reinterpretations above,
   which is where an address and the word holding it already met.

   `null` needs its type from context - an assignment, a return, or an ascription - which is why the
   test exists separately: `isNull(p)` needs nothing.

   Neither costs a call. Both are one expression over their parameters with no call left in it, which
   is the shape the resolver folds at the site rather than emitting - see `foldTrivialBody`. That is
   a general rule about bodies of this shape rather than a rule about these two, so a program's own
   `fn isEmpty(xs: [a]) -> Bool = length(xs) == 0` gets the same treatment with no optimizer having
   run.

   `0 :: Size` and not a bare `0`, because there are two rungs to an address - `Size` and `USize` -
   and a literal with nothing to settle it would name neither.
-}
@inline(always) pub fn null() -> %a = bitcast(0 :: Size)
@inline(always) pub fn isNull(it: %a) -> Bool = (bitcast(it) :: Size) == 0

{-
   A borrow of what a pointer names.

   The one operation that re-enters the checked world from the unchecked one. Nothing here verified
   that the pointer is valid, that what it names is initialized, or that nothing else writes through
   it while the borrow is live - which is exactly what makes it Native's and not the language's. A
   collection written over raw storage needs it, and having written it, owes its callers a signature
   whose origin qualifier says what the result is rooted in.

   **The tick is on the pointee**, which is the reading `'` has everywhere else: it goes on the type
   constructor's argument, so `%source'a` is a pointer to storage in `source` and `source'(%a)` would
   be a pointer *field* read through a borrow of somebody's record. Both mean something and they are
   not the same thing, and this is the first. It replaces the `return it:` marker, which said the
   same thing in a spelling only a parameter had.
-}
@builtin(pointerBorrow)
pub fn borrow(it: %source'a) -> source'a
@builtin(pointerBorrowMut)
pub fn borrowMut(it: %source'a) -> &source'a

{-
   What this target made of a type: everything raw-pointer code needs to know about a layout it did
   not choose.

   `@platform(native)`, and the only declarations in this file that need to say so - the rest of it
   is raw pointers, which no managed target has at all. A byte count is a fact about a layout *this
   compiler chose*, and on a managed target it did not choose one: how much a value costs is the
   runtime's answer, it changes with the engine's hidden classes and its collector, and nothing a
   program emits there can observe it. So the question has no answer rather than a different one,
   and a managed type descriptor carries no measurement to load - see ManagedTypeDesc.

   **The type comes from the position, not from a value.** `traits() :: TypeTraits(Entry(k, v))`
   asks about a type nothing has an instance of, which is what the `sizeOf(it: a)` this replaced
   could not do at all: its parameter existed only so that `a` had somewhere to come from, so a
   layout question about a type had to be phrased as a question about a value, and code that had no
   value in hand made one up. It binds `a` from the result exactly as `null() :: %U8` binds a
   pointee.

   **`stride` is not `size`, and that is the field this exists for.** A record's trailing padding
   belongs to whoever contains it, so `size` is what a whole-value copy moves and `stride` is what
   the *next* element starts at. `data P {a: I64, b: U8}` is nine bytes and sixteen apart. Code that
   walks storage by `size` overlaps its own elements, and until this record there was no way to ask
   for the other number - which is why `byteSpan` below is written as a pointer difference.

   **The two flags are facts, never permissions.** They say what this compiler decided about `a`,
   so a program may read them to pick between two paths that do the same thing - a block copy
   against an element loop, a teardown walk against nothing - and may not read them to decide what
   it owns. Ownership is what the `Copy` and `Drop` constraints in a signature say, and a body
   compiled against an unconstrained `a` is checked as though both answers were the pessimistic one
   whichever way these come out at one call site.
-}
@platform(native) pub data TypeTraits(a) {
    -- What one value occupies, and what a whole-value copy moves. Not rounded up to the alignment.
    size: Size,
    align: Size,

    -- What indexing homogeneous storage advances by: `size` rounded up to `align`, and larger than
    -- `size` for a record whose last field leaves padding.
    stride: Size,

    trivialCopy: Bool,
    hasTeardown: Bool
}

@builtin(typeTraits)
@platform(native) pub fn traits() -> TypeTraits(a)

{-
   The same question asked about a value in hand - `traitsOf(q.tag).size`.

   Written here rather than supplied by the compiler, and that is the point of the split: `traits`
   is the one thing the language cannot say about itself, and "the type of this expression" is
   something it says everywhere. So the convenience is a line of source and there is one intrinsic.

   The parameter is `@lazy`-free and shared, so nothing is evaluated for its size beyond what
   naming it costs; a caller that has no value writes `traits()` and the type.
-}
@platform(native) pub fn traitsOf(it: a) -> TypeTraits(a) = traits()

{-
   The three single measurements, for the reading where naming the record is more than the question
   is worth: `sizeOf(q.tag)` says what it means and `traitsOf(q.tag).size` says it twice.

   One line each over `traitsOf`, and that is the whole of what they are - there is no second
   mechanism here, no second intrinsic, and nothing the compiler knows about them. What they cost is
   what the field read costs, which is nothing: every component of a `traits()` is a metric that
   folds to an immediate at a known type, and the record dissolves wherever it is used.

   `sizeOf` and `alignOf` were the *primitives* before `TypeTraits` existed, and asking them about a
   type meant manufacturing a value of it. They are conveniences over the primitive now, so the
   question about a type is `traits() :: TypeTraits(T)` and these are the question about a value.
   `strideOf` joins them because the third measurement is the one raw-pointer code gets wrong.
-}
@platform(native) pub fn sizeOf(it: a) -> Size = traitsOf(it).size
@platform(native) pub fn alignOf(it: a) -> Size = traitsOf(it).align
@platform(native) pub fn strideOf(it: a) -> Size = traitsOf(it).stride

{-
   Pointer arithmetic, in elements rather than in bytes: `p + 1` advances by one `a`, whatever an
   `a` is. Byte-granular work casts to `%U8` first, where the two coincide.

   **The result refers to what the argument referred to** - `%source'a` at both ends. An element of a
   window is `borrow(values(xs) + i)`, and the borrow is rooted wherever the pointer says its pointee
   is: `values` published `xs`'s loan on its result, and this is what carries it across the offset. A
   plain `%a` result carries nothing, so the borrow would be one no signature can say whose storage
   it is - and `op.def`'s `pointerAdd` row now requires the qualifier rather than leaving the two
   readings to drift.

   **The count is any integer**, and not `Size` alone. A plain function cannot be overloaded, so a
   second `+` taking `USize` was not a thing this module could declare, and a `U32` index was the one
   an author had to launder through `bitcast(n :: USize) :: Size` - two conversions stating nothing
   either meant. The compiler widens the count to the address's width before scaling it, keeping its
   sign: a signed count is sign-extended to `Size` and an unsigned one zero-extended to `USize`. A
   `Vec` satisfies `Integral` too and is refused at the call.
-}
@builtin(pointerAdd)
pub fn (Integral(n)) +(it: %source'a, count: n) -> %source'a
@builtin(pointerSub)
pub fn (Integral(n)) -(it: %source'a, count: n) -> %source'a
@builtin(pointerDifference)
pub fn difference(from: %a, to: %a) -> Size
